     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. A single dose of 1600 mg is well above ibuprofen's usual per-dose ceiling of 800 mg. At 6,400 mg/day, the total is twice the maximum daily dose. I don't know of a clinical indication that justifies it. The extra dose adds toxicity, including GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular risk, without adding analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit. Analgesia plateaus at lower doses.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the maximum.** For example, 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day) or 600 mg PO four times daily (2,400 mg/day). I'll enter either one now if you tell me which.\n- **Add other analgesia.** If pain control is the goal, I can enter scheduled acetaminophen alongside ibuprofen. I can also enter another option you choose.\n- **Review the dose with a pharmacist or your attending.** If you have a specific reason for this dose, they can review it. It would need to go through their process, not through me.\n\nI can also enter a different order if the dose or frequency was a typo, for example 1600 mg/day divided into 400 mg four times daily."
      }
